WEBVTT

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.680
Welcome back to another episode of Paragliding Atlas Podcast.

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:07.000
I'm your host, Aninda Singh, and joining us today is none other

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.240
than the man himself, Nick Nenes.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.840
Nick, what an absolute pleasure to have you on the show, man.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:19.360
Thank you so much for coming. Thank you, it's been a while

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.360
since I've been on a Perry clothing podcast but.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.840
Yeah, Nick, you know, I have been wanting to get you on the

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.520
show since I started the show. And I've been like, you know,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.040
I've been aware. I've been admiring your

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:32.960
lifestyle, the way you fly and everything.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.160
But most importantly, something that has made me very curious

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.600
and intrigued about you is, is that your Instagram handle or

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.640
YouTube handle? I don't know.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.440
It's got a very cool name. It says share my joys.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:46.920
God damn, dude. What's the story behind that

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.360
name? So I stole it.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.680
It was a book that I read from a mountaineering God that I never

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:01.320
met, but like someone, I don't know, maybe in the 50s or

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.880
sometimes before I was born. And, and that was the title of

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.040
the book. So I just stole it.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.800
But yeah, they were just talking about all their their adventures

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.880
in in the mountains and that kind of thing in in New Zealand

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:14.680
as well. Yeah.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.280
Like for for the perspective of our listeners, you know, the the

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:22.040
show is 2 years old and I think you you're the most active years

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.360
of you in in paragliding were a little bit earlier than that.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.720
So for the pilots who have just practised the out of flight not

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.040
that long ago, a little introduction about you if you

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:37.080
can. Yeah, so I learnt to fly in 2007

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.160
and I learnt in the southeast corner of Queensland, like the

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:48.240
eastern part of Australia. And the first thing I did was

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.120
like, I read about how light the wings were on the Internet and I

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:55.200
thought you could take these into the mountains and, and fly

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.360
by yourself. And then I talked to the

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:04.560
instructor in Kanungra where I learnt and I said, can can you

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.200
really like, what's the story? And he said, first you've got to

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.160
learn how to fly. I learnt in May 2007 and by the

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.200
end of the year I was doing my first flights in New Zealand,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.800
literally jumping over the fence at the back of Mum's place and

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:23.240
knocking up the hill. That's such a cool training

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.680
ground, I would say, because the excitement and the rush that you

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.160
have as a new beginner pilot, like somebody who has just got

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.800
in the sport is like you can't get your you can't get enough of

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.080
it right. So if you have something that I

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.800
set up like that in your backyard, I think that must have

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.320
contributed to your better initial progression

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.480
comparatively to others. Yeah, so, so for me, the the

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:51.720
main reason I got into flying was not really from aviation

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.080
side or a love of flight or just wanting to fly no matter what or

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.720
whatever. It was more that I just wanted

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.720
to explore places and that was a tool to do it.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.520
But it was it was really hot. I can fly.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.760
That was really interesting to me.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:08.000
And since then when I started learning flying, I realized that

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.360
you can actually take off and go up as well.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:15.880
And, and you can not just climb up something and fly down South.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.200
Yeah. Then then I learned a lot more

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.920
about the air itself and. And that was also an interesting

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.160
part of it. But the start of it was just

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.680
exploring country that I already knew.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.320
Like I'd spend a lot of time looking at maps.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.840
I'd done a lot of trips just on foot, like we call it tramping

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.640
in New Zealand, visiting a lot of these places.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.480
And there was one time I always tell a story where I, I climbed

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:43.600
up from the valley and I got almost up to this Alpine lake,

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:49.120
but the Bush got really thick. And it was so thick that I had

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:53.000
to take off my pack and drag it after me and to go 30 meters.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.920
That was, you know, very steep. But it was very short distance,

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:01.160
like 100 feet, Took me 30 meters, took me 30 minutes.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:06.960
So when you're used to that kind of travel time in your

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:09.280
mountains, depending on what it's like, it's nice to be able

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.040
to think that you can just fly straight over the top.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:14.160
So yeah, I spent a lot of time looking at maps and just

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:17.000
dreaming about trips I could do. Like, I would say that for me,

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.120
paragliding had three parts to it.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.240
One was thinking about flights you could do, and another one

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.760
was doing the flights, and the other one was going back and

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:31.640
thinking about what you've done. And that's what I've spent a lot

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.120
of time video editing. And that was an enjoyable part

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.920
of it. Sometimes you feel like, oh, how

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:37.120
am I going to get through all this footage?

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.040
But usually when you get started it's quite fun reviewing what

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:47.640
you you did and seeing if if the flight was actually like you

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.720
thought it was when you landed, if it was maybe different to how

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:53.680
you remembered. A a quick thought comes from my

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:56.360
mind because you mentioned that, you know, the anxiety that comes

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.200
with all the footage that we capture because it's it's so

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.360
huge and editing is such a skill that yeah, you need to put in a

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.040
lot of work towards it. Like sometimes I, I think I've

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:09.320
told this on the show before that editing a 62nd trailer for

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.640
this show takes almost like 6-7 hours.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.520
So it, it takes a huge chunk out of your name.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:18.880
I was just kind of wondering like only if the hardware

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.880
manufacturers could make, what do you say the software as well.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:26.360
So user friendly that because right now everybody is able to

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:30.680
operate Gopro's because they're so easy to operate, but almost

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:35.120
like 95% of them don't know what to do with the footage because

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:37.680
it's so damn hard to edit the footage.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.960
And eventually like you buy the camera out of excitement because

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:44.040
it looks fancy. And all the adverts that you see

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:49.760
are very professionally edited. But I have seen almost 95% of

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:53.120
the pilots just letting their GoPro eat dust after like the

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:55.160
first couple of weeks or something because they don't

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.560
know what to do with it anymore afterwards.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:00.160
So. I agree.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.240
I've got two things to say about that.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:12.920
One is that like the fact that I edited a lot of my footage gave

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:16.600
me feedback to not take as much footage and just to do short

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:19.720
clips. So when I made the video about

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:22.800
my accident, a lot of people said how come you didn't include

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.520
the accident and was like, well, I don't record all the time.

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:31.840
I just record, you know, it, one turn in a thermal or say a few

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.440
words when I'm on the glide somewhere, and just do it when

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.400
something interesting or different is happening.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:39.600
I don't like record the whole thing because it would be too

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.400
much work to go through it all. Plus you've got to have the card

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:43.520
space and the battery and all that.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:47.880
So you're having the feedback of having to edit means that I was

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:51.040
a lot more deliberate and succinct when I was making the

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:52.640
footage to make that part easier.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:07:00.240
Because I, yeah, I was thinking about making really nice videos.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:03.560
Like what? Like around the time of the

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:06.000
pandemic, I thought I'll get some decent cameras and I'll do

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.680
a decent job because a lot of the time I was making videos

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:10.880
just basically for my own memories.

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:16.160
But, you know, Ben Callard and and Ben Jordan, they make videos

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:20.000
which are really nice, but they spend an awful lot of time doing

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:21.440
it. Or at least.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.200
Yeah. So there's a lot of, you know,

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:29.560
having different cameras and, and not being as fast on the

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:32.280
ground, like maybe missing a couple of flights because you

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:35.480
spent too much time trying to set the cameras up or whatever.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:40.480
I don't know. So yeah, what was my second

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.840
point that yeah, I was basically doing it as a story and not as

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:46.320
a, you know, amazing experience for the user.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.480
It's great if people get something out of watching the

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:51.720
videos, but it's it's more for me just to remember it.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:56.440
And yeah, anyway, so I never got on to making those really good

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:59.960
videos because I had the accident after the pandemic but

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:02.240
before I got to get go back to New Zealand.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:06.880
But it's leave all that hard work up to those other guys.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:13.000
You know, I think you mentioned two people who have actually

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:16.200
invested. They're like, you know, a lot of

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:19.400
pilots, they invest their time, energy and effort into chasing

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:20.880
distance of getting better and flying.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:24.120
But these are like 2 guys. I mean, I'm sure there are many

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.280
others, but these are two guys that stand out when it comes to

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:30.720
the amount of effort they invest in bringing you along their

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:33.799
adventures. In case any of the viewers are

00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:35.159
wondering who we are talking about.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.000
Yeah, just just look up for Benjamin in the index of this

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:43.240
show and you will find both of them have been on Paragliding

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:45.440
Atlas. So there are episodes with

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:49.120
Benjamin Jordan and Benjamin Kellett where they spill all the

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:52.120
secrets on how they bring this awesomeness to your screens.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:56.440
But Nick, I was going to come to your accident eventually because

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:04.560
it is kind of, you know, how do I say a looming possibility that

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:08.240
kind of sticks around at any given point of time in life, in

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:12.080
instance, in paragliding life is what I meant.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:16.960
After how many years of flying did that accident happen?

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:20.880
And how has flying looked like for you afterwards?

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:27.840
OK, so I learnt in 2007 and I had my accident 15 years after

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:29.760
that. I had actually been gliding

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:34.720
since the pandemic, so I'd started doing that as well.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:39.480
But yeah, I had a lot of adventures in in 15 years.

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:41.480
So that's three and a bit years ago.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:48.440
And yeah, after that I haven't really had an interest in

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:52.560
paragliding. I think it is a great activity

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:55.720
because I'm a paraplegic, so waist up is OK for a lot of

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:58.880
paraplegic people. Paragliding might be a great

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:00.760
activity, but it depends what you want to get out of it.

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:05.000
And for me, the main things that were special about paragliding

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:08.400
were the independence that you could do everything on your own

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:10.440
and that you could take off at land just about anywhere.

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:13.280
So that's not really applicable if you're paraplegic.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:16.920
But if you just want to take off, fly a local site or fly

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:21.080
coastal, it can be great. But gliding since I before the

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:26.480
the year before the accident, I started gliding in a French club

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:29.400
that already has gliders that are set up for disabled people

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:32.160
because you use your feet for the rudders in a normal glider.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:35.040
But there is a modified ones. Yeah, luckily.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:38.480
Exactly. Sail playing gliders.

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.520
Yep. So I could fly with those guys

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:45.600
again after the accident at the same club, but evolved a lot

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:49.480
more paperwork and bureaucracy to get my medical and to get all

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:52.040
that drama going. It took a lot of effort.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:55.080
So paragording is the other advantage of it is it's

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:59.200
relatively straightforward in terms of aviation regulations

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:02.560
and rules and stuff like that. Sometimes I was attracted

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:06.280
towards base because of how unregulated it is, because I

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:11.880
somehow feel that humans thrive evaluating risk best when they

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:14.160
don't have anybody to fall back on.

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:16.120
When they know that nobody's got their back.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:19.680
It's like, yeah, the buck stops with me because you are the ones

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:21.400
who's going to reap the rewards and you are the ones who's going

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:24.280
to pay the price if you do something out of the spectrum.

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:28.840
Regulations are always a bit of a hit and miss in my head as

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:32.920
like yes, when it comes to masses, education is important

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:35.040
because otherwise you won't guide them correctly.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:40.360
But when it comes to the niches, which is like selected few, I

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:45.440
think chasing excellence can only come when you are managing

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:49.280
the drive within you according to your own abilities.

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:53.440
Rather than having paperwork put some kind of a structure

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:58.160
definition around it which changes your perception of risk.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:02.120
It's like risk perception as a stand alone person versus risk

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:03.760
perception being a part of the system.

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:07.440
Yeah. Commercial aviation, it's

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:10.040
probably justified that there is a lot of regulation if they've

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:13.920
got a lot of layers of catching mistakes so they don't turn into

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:17.320
accidents. Paragliding, the weight of the

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:21.920
aircraft is just a few kilos and your speed is one of the slowest

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:25.120
aircrafts. So yeah, it makes sense that you

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:28.080
don't regulate that as much. And gliding, I think it makes

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:30.000
sense that it's in between those two extremes.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.640
Europe is quite highly regulated compared to other countries.

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:35.480
Yeah. The thing is the the gliding

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:38.000
clubs aren't really the ones helping you to get through it

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:40.720
all, but it's the association that's all over Europe.

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:43.160
So it combines all the different countries together and to get

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:44.680
anything changed as a real dramas.

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:46.680
It is what it is. In terms of gliding, I think

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:49.600
Australia is a lot more straightforward.

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:53.560
From what I hear, you can have an experimental category there

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:57.120
for the glottis, which makes things there's a lot more

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.920
flexibility. But New Zealand is probably

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:01.760
somewhere in between Australia and Europe.

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:07.320
OK, so regulating one example that comes to my mind which of a

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:09.800
flawed regulation is let's say the EN standard.

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:14.160
Time and again we have seen the whole crowd going about how

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:18.280
ineffective this is and how it gives you a false perception and

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:21.560
people are making decisions based on this standard, whereas

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:25.320
it is only supposed to educate you on very specific parameters

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:29.240
because they are the only ones we are able to measure in a

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:31.720
controlled environment. Any other parameter is not

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:34.600
measurable. So I feel like there's a pseudo

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:38.200
safety that comes in people's mind with a certain bit of

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:40.720
regulation. Even though a lot of

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:44.040
manufacturers and top test pilots say that certification is

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:46.320
not supposed to be taken as something to choose the wing.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:52.400
But because that regulation exists and the audience below it

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:55.640
who is looking up to that regulation is like, let's say a

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:57.560
new pilot, he's going to go to his instructor and the

00:13:57.560 --> 00:13:59.800
instructor is going to see how the industry standard is.

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:02.360
So even the instructor are saying that yes, it's AE and a

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:03.680
glider. That's why you should buy it.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.280
That depends on how qualified the instructor is, how much his

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:10.400
experience is because at the level of teaching instructors,

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:14.200
the regulation is very different compared to the regulation

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:16.160
controlling how the wings are produced.

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:21.040
I feel that pseudo safety messes up with your risk management

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:23.000
because right now the student has OK.

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:26.720
My instructor has my 100% trust because he has been flying for a

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:31.120
while and instructor thinks that the EN certification has 100%

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:33.280
trust because it has been in place for a while.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:37.920
But the bottom line is that the instructor is not even close to

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:40.040
answering the question that both the student and instructor are

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:41.680
seeking. So.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:45.280
Yeah. But did he talk with Luke Hamont

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.280
about this? And he said you shouldn't look

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:49.800
at the EN rating and you should look at what the manufacturer

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:52.120
says about it because they're the ones pitching it to a

